    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class (the others are a base and a helper). The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same firstName and lastName, case-insensitively \u2014 on `POST /api/owners`, returned via the owner DTO.\n\nChanges:\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `namesakeCount` field (`namesake_count` column) with getter/setter, alongside the existing `customerCode`/`householdId`.\n- **New function `AssignNamesakeCount.java`** \u2014 counts existing owners matching the new owner's firstName+lastName (case-insensitive, trimmed) via `OwnerRepository.findAll()` and sets the count on the owner in place. It runs after `BuildOwner` (owner exists) and before `SaveOwner` (new owner not yet counted), mirroring the existing `AssignCustomerCode`/`AssignHousehold` steps.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `assignNamesakeCount` step into the pipeline under the create transaction.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` property to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` carries and returns it.\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 ignored `namesakeCount` when mapping the request `OwnerFieldsDto` \u2192 `Owner` (it's server-computed, not a request field).\n- **Schemas** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `namesake_count INTEGER` column to the `owners` table.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` test and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
